  "subtitle": "Wall Street's dedicated quote terminal: type a ticker, press a labeled field key, read the green glass",
  "why_scout_cares": "Before Bloomberg there was Quotron: a dedicated, non-QWERTY market-data terminal whose entire interface is a custom keyboard of ticker-symbol and field keys (LAST, BID, ASK, VOL...) driving a green-phosphor CRT. No cursor, no mouse — pure key-driven retrieval from a central computer. At 60% of the 1986 financial-data market and 100,000 rented terminals, it is the professional terminal against which every later trading terminal was measured, immortalized in the 1987 film Wall Street. Fills the museum's professional market-data terminal gap — distinct from Minitel (consumer videotex), Famicom Network System (home finance), and all other network exhibits.",
  "search_terms": ["Quotron", "Quotron II", "market data terminal", "Scantlin Electronics", "stock quote terminal 1980s", "green phosphor CRT terminal"],
